<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Penn</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1644–1718</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Penn was an English Quaker and the founder of the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>. Son of the admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sir-william-penn">Sir William Penn</a>, he became a trustee of West Jersey and a proprietor of East Jersey, and in 1681 Charles II granted him a vast colony west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delaware-river">Delaware River</a> to settle a debt owed to his father. He planned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> and guaranteed religious freedom; in 1682 he also received the Lower Counties that became Delaware.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-penn.jpg" alt="William Penn" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artistUnknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Penn.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
